/-!
# Garnett–Marshall, Appendix F: Martingales, Brownian motion, and Kakutani's theorem

Source: J. B. Garnett, D. E. Marshall, *Harmonic Measure*, Cambridge University Press 2005, Appendix F (pp. 470–480;
`full.md` P3:3849–4373: the main text to P3:4206, Exercises F.1–F.3 at P3:4208–4372).

Conventions. A probability space `(X, ℳ, P)` is a type `X` with a σ-algebra `m0` and a measure `P` with
`IsProbabilityMeasure P`; a sub-σ-algebra `𝒩 ⊂ ℳ` is `m ≤ m0`. Functions are complex valued (leader ruling Fa6): the
book's `L¹` is complex ("We may suppose `f` is real", P3:4021, is a reduction inside a proof, and the walk (F.9) is a
complex martingale); `f ∈ L^p(X, ℳ, P)` is `MemLp f p P` and `‖f‖_p` is `eLpNorm f p P`, in `[0, ∞]`. The
conditional expectation `E(f∣𝒩)` is Mathlib's `P[f | m]`, a martingale is Mathlib's `Martingale`, a stopping time is
Mathlib's `IsStoppingTime` (the `exists` bullets below). The increasing σ-algebras `ℳ₁ ⊂ ℳ₂ ⊂ … ⊂ ℳ` are a Mathlib
`Filtration ℕ m0`, indexed from `0` (the book's `ℳ_{n+1}` is `ℱ n`; the walk's own filtration starts, as the book's,
with the trivial `ℳ₀`). The book's standing condition (F.2), "`⋃_n ℳ_n` generates `ℳ`", is the hypothesis
`⨆ n, ℱ n = m0`, carried only by the statements whose proofs use it, Lemma F.2 and Theorem F.5; Lemma F.3, Corollary
F.4 and Doob's theorem are stated for any filtration, a documented true generalization (leader ruling Fa6: App. J
reuses them for dyadic filtrations on `ℝ`, which do not generate the Borel σ-algebra of `ℝ`, and an unneeded (F.2)
would block those uses). "Weakly in `L^p`" is `∫ f_n h dP → ∫ f h dP` for every `h ∈ L^q`, `q = p/(p − 1)`. The
maximal function `f*(x) = sup_n |f_n(x)|` is written inline in `[0, ∞]`. From P3:4041 on, `Ω` is a bounded domain
of `ℂ` (open, connected, bounded), `dist(z, ∂Ω)` is `Metric.infDist z (frontier Ω)`, and `ω(z₀, E, Ω)` is Wiener's
harmonic measure `harmonicMeasureWiener (toExt Ω) z₀` of Ch. III §5 (leader rulings D1, B5: an arbitrary bounded
domain), evaluated at the plane Borel set `toExt E` and compared in `[0, ∞]` with probabilities; `Cap(ℂ* \ Ω) > 0`
holds automatically for bounded `Ω` and is not added. Limits such as `z_∞ = lim z_n` enter as
`∃ w, Tendsto … (𝓝 w) ∧ …`, never through `limUnder` (leader ruling Fa8). The uniform law on `[0, 2π)` is Mathlib's
`volume[|Set.Ico 0 (2 * π)]` (leader ruling Fa8/Fb6; no helper). The book prints `w(z₀, E, Ω)` and `dw(z₀, ζ)` for
`ω` in four places (P3:4107, 4113, 4137, 4195; the equation images show `w`), read as `ω`.

* Definition (the conditional expectation `F = E(f∣𝒩)` of `f ∈ L¹(X, ℳ, P)` given a sub-σ-algebra `𝒩 ⊂ ℳ`, App.
  F, P3:3851–3861) — exists: `MeasureTheory.condExp` (Mathlib's P[f | m] for m ≤ m0, for functions with values in
  a Banach space such as ℂ; for integrable f it is m-strongly measurable and satisfies (F.1), see the next two
  bullets; its value 0 for non-integrable f is outside the book's discourse f ∈ L¹)
* Unlabelled (App. F, P3:3851–3867, the existence part of (F.1); used through every later `E(·∣ℳ_n)`): for
  `f ∈ L¹` there is an `𝒩`-measurable `F` with (F.1) `∫ χ_S f dP = ∫ χ_S F dP` for all `S ∈ 𝒩` — exists:
  `MeasureTheory.stronglyMeasurable_condExp`, `MeasureTheory.setIntegral_condExp` (F = P[f | 𝒩] is 𝒩-strongly
  measurable, the first name, and for integrable f and S in 𝒩 its integral over S is that of f, the second; the
  hypothesis SigmaFinite (P.trim hm) of the second is an instance for a finite measure)
* Unlabelled (App. F, P3:3863–3867, the uniqueness part of (F.1); cited at P3:3911, "From the uniqueness of
  conditional expectations it follows that (F.4)"): `F` is unique almost everywhere — exists:
  `MeasureTheory.ae_eq_condExp_of_forall_setIntegral_eq` (an a.e. 𝒩-strongly measurable G, integrable on the sets of
  𝒩, whose integral over every S in 𝒩 is that of the integrable f, equals P[f | 𝒩] almost everywhere; the book's F
  is such a G, since the integrals in (F.1) exist)
* Example F.1 (App. F, P3:3871–3891) — omitted: an example (for the σ-algebra generated by a partition of [0, 2π)
  into intervals, E(f∣𝒩) is the step function of the averages of f), not cited as a result; Exercise F.2
  re-derives its dyadic form
* Definition (martingale relative to increasing σ-algebras `ℳ₁ ⊂ ℳ₂ ⊂ … ⊂ ℳ`, (F.3) `f_n = E(f_{n+1}∣ℳ_n)`,
  App. F, P3:3893–3909; the generation condition (F.2) is a hypothesis of the statements that use it) — exists:
  `MeasureTheory.Martingale` (ℕ-indexed and ℂ-valued, relative to a Mathlib Filtration ℕ m0, leader ruling Fa6; the
  book's one-step condition (F.3), with f_n ℳ_n-measurable and integrable, is equivalent to Mathlib's condition,
  strongly adapted with P[f j | ℱ i] = f i almost everywhere for all i ≤ j: Mathlib's martingale_nat gives one
  direction, (F.4) with Martingale.integrable and Martingale.stronglyMeasurable the other; for ℂ-valued functions
  measurability and strong measurability agree)
* Formula (F.4) (App. F, P3:3911–3915): `f_n = E(f_{n+k}∣ℳ_n)`, `k > 0` — exists:
  `MeasureTheory.Martingale.condExp_ae_eq` (P[f j | ℱ i] = f i almost everywhere for i ≤ j, here j = n + k)
* Formula (F.5) (App. F, P3:3919–3923): for `f ∈ L¹(X, ℳ, P)`, `f_n = E(f∣ℳ_n)` is a martingale — exists:
  `MeasureTheory.martingale_condExp` (n ↦ P[f | ℱ n] is a martingale; its SigmaFiniteFiltration hypothesis is an
  instance for finite measures, and Mathlib needs no integrability of f)
* Lemma F.2 (App. F) — `lemmaF_2`
* Doob's martingale theorem (named, quoted without proof, App. F, P3:3949) — `doob_martingale_theorem`
* Lemma F.3 (App. F; (F.6), the weak-`L¹` estimate for the maximal function) — `lemmaF_3`
* Definition (stopping time, `{N(x) ≤ n} ∈ ℳ_n`, App. F, P3:3995) — exists: `MeasureTheory.IsStoppingTime` (the
  set where τ ≤ i is ℱ i-measurable for every i; the values in WithTop ℕ admit the book's N(x) = inf ∅ = ∞)
* Corollary F.4 (App. F) — `corF_4`
* Theorem F.5 (App. F; "the weak limit provided by Lemma F.3" is Lemma F.2 [misprint]) — `thmF_5`
* Definition (the martingale (F.9) `z_{n+1} = z_n + ½ dist(z_n, ∂Ω) e^{iθ_{n+1}}` on the infinite product
  `X = {(e^{iθ₁}, e^{iθ₂}, …)}` of unit circles with the product measure `P = ∏ dθ_j/2π` and the σ-algebras
  `ℳ₀ = {∅, X}`, `ℳ_n = σ(θ₁, …, θ_n)`, App. F, P3:4041–4069) — `circleWalkMeasure`, `circleWalkFiltration`,
  `circleWalk`
* Unlabelled (App. F, P3:4071–4075): `{z_n}` is a martingale relative to `{ℳ_n}`, because
  `(1/2π) ∫₀^{2π} e^{iθ} dθ = 0` — `circleWalk_martingale`
* Unlabelled (App. F, P3:4082–4086): because `Ω` is bounded, the martingale theorem ensures that `z_∞ = lim_n z_n`
  exists almost everywhere on `X` — `circleWalk_tendsto_ae`
* Unlabelled (App. F, P3:4088–4094): because `dist(z_n, ∂Ω) = 2|z_{n+1} − z_n| → 0`, `z_∞ ∈ ∂Ω` whenever `z_∞`
  exists — `circleWalk_limit_mem_frontier`
* Theorem F.6 (Kakutani, App. F; (F.10)) — `thmF_6`
* Definition (Brownian motion started at `z₀`: the probability space `(W, ℳ, P)` of continuous paths `B(t)`,
  `t ≥ 0`, with `B(0) = z₀`, App. F, P3:4142–4166) — `IsPlanarBrownianFrom`
* Wiener's theorem (named, quoted without proof, App. F, P3:4142, "By a theorem of Wiener") —
  `wiener_brownian_motion`
* Definition (`τ_∞ = inf {t : B(t) ∈ ∂Ω}`, App. F, P3:4180) — exists: `MeasureTheory.hittingAfter` (at the process
  B, the set ∂Ω = frontier Ω of ℂ and the starting time 0: the infimum of the times t ≥ 0 with B(t) in ∂Ω, and ⊤ if
  ∂Ω is never hit; values in WithTop ℝ≥0)
* Unlabelled (App. F, P3:4192–4198): Theorem F.6 restated for Brownian motion, `ω(z₀, E, Ω) = P(B(τ_∞) ∈ E)` —
  `thmF_6_brownian`

No auxiliary theorems: the two proof obligations of `circleWalkFiltration` (the fields `mono'` and `le'` of a
`Filtration`) are discharged by genuine proofs inside the definition (leader ruling Fa7).

Not stated (inventory U−, remarks and proof steps): "We will have `F ≠ f`, unless `f` happens to be
`𝒩`-measurable", and, for `f ∈ L²`, `F` is the orthogonal projection of `f` onto `L²(X, 𝒩, P)` (P3:3869, remarks,
not cited; Mathlib's `MeasureTheory.condExpL2` is the projection); the quoted theorem "a martingale has the form
(F.5) if and only if `{f_n}` is uniformly integrable" (P3:3925, quoted without a label or a name and not cited;
Lemma F.2 is the weak form the book needs; near misses Mathlib `MeasureTheory.Martingale.ae_eq_condExp_limitProcess`
and `MeasureTheory.Integrable.uniformIntegrable_condExp_filtration`); the proof of Lemma F.3 (`N(x)`, `S_n`, (F.7)),
with its claim that `N(x) = inf {n : |f_n(x)| > λ}` is a stopping time (P3:3969–3995); the proofs of Corollary F.4
and of Theorem F.5 (`Ω_f`, (F.8), the dense class `𝒟`, Egoroff's theorem; P3:3999–4039); the description of the
uniform distribution `P(z_{n+1} ∈ A) = length(A)/length(Γ(z_n))` (P3:4047–4057, built into `circleWalkMeasure`); the
remark that (F.9) implies `z_{n+1} ∈ Ω` and `z_{n+1} ≠ z_n` (P3:4077, immediate, not cited); the strip example
`Ω = {Re z > 0, |Im z| < 1}` with `ω(0, E, Ω) ≤ C₁ e^{−c₂ n}` and the precise asymptotics (P3:4104–4116, an example,
not cited); the proof of Theorem F.6 (the martingale `u(z_n)` for harmonic `u`, (F.11) through Kellogg's theorem
= Corollary III.6.4 and Evans' theorem = Theorem E.2, P3:4118–4140); the book's list of properties of Brownian
motion (circles are hit almost surely, the first hit is uniformly distributed, the increments are stationary and
independent; P3:4142–4164: consequences of the normalization, see `wiener_brownian_motion`); the term "Brownian
path" (P3:4166); the embedding of the walk in Brownian motion, `τ₁ < τ₂ < ⋯`, `z_n = B(τ_n)`, `τ_∞ = lim τ_n` and
`B(τ_∞) = z_∞` (P3:4168–4190, the book's justification of the restatement); the closing remark that (F.10) cannot
serve as an alternate definition of the solution of the Dirichlet problem (P3:4200–4206). Figure F.1 is not an item.
Exercises F.1–F.3 (P3:4208–4372) are not items; Exercise F.2(c) defines BMO on `∂𝔻`, which Ch. VII §5 defines as a
notion of the book (leader ruling D3; VII, P2:2871, "Recall from Appendix F that f has bounded mean oscillation"),
and (F.12) prints `|f − f_n| < λ` inside `E(χ ∣ ℳ_n)` for `> λ` [misprint], recorded only. Other citations into this
appendix: Ch. III, P1:3274 and P1:3278 (Kakutani's theorem and the Brownian approach, `thmF_6`,
`thmF_6_brownian`); Ch. X, Exercise 15 (P3:2199–2245: Brownian paths, "By Kakutani's theorem", "the independence of
Brownian increments"; an exercise, which uses `thmF_6_brownian` and properties of `IsPlanarBrownianFrom`); App. J
(P3:5249–5630: martingales, stopping times, "by the martingale theorem" at P3:5382, `doob_martingale_theorem`).
-/

