namespace GarnettMarshall2005

open LiZhongQC (ExtPlane toExt)

/-- **Lemma F.2** (App. F, P3:3927–3947; cited as the source of the weak limit in Theorem F.5, P3:4013 (printed
"Lemma F.3" [misprint]), and in the proof of Theorem F.6, P3:4134). Let `1 < p < ∞` and let `{f_n}` be a martingale
relative to `{ℳ_n}` such that `sup_n ‖f_n‖_p < ∞`. Then there is `f ∈ L^p(X, ℳ, P)` such that `f_n = E(f∣ℳ_n)`
and `f_n` converges to `f` weakly in `L^p`.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa6): `(X, ℳ, P)` a probability space and `{ℳ_n}` a filtration (indexed from `0`) with
(F.2) `⨆ n, ℱ n = m0`, the book's standing condition, which the proof uses ("By (F.2) the limit point `f` does not
depend on the subsequence"); complex-valued functions; `sup_n ‖f_n‖_p` in `[0, ∞]`; `f_n = E(f∣ℳ_n)` almost
everywhere (conditional expectations are determined almost everywhere); "weakly in `L^p`" is `∫ f_n h dP → ∫ f h dP`
for every `h ∈ L^q`, `q = p/(p − 1)` (the dual pairing of `L^p`, `1 < p < ∞`; the proof tests with `χ_S ∈ L^q`), and
these integrals exist by Hölder's inequality. -/
theorem lemmaF_2 {X : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace X} {P : Measure X} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0} (hgen : ⨆ n, ℱ n = m0) {f : ℕ → X → ℂ} (hf : Martingale f ℱ P) {p : ℝ} (hp : 1 < p)
    (hbdd : ⨆ n, eLpNorm (f n) (ENNReal.ofReal p) P < ⊤) :
    ∃ g : X → ℂ, MemLp g (ENNReal.ofReal p) P ∧ (∀ n, f n =ᵐ[P] P[g | ℱ n]) ∧
      ∀ h : X → ℂ, MemLp h (ENNReal.ofReal (p / (p - 1))) P →
        Tendsto (fun n => ∫ x, f n x * h x ∂P) atTop (𝓝 (∫ x, g x * h x ∂P)) := by
  sorry

/-- **Doob's martingale theorem** (App. F, P3:3949, named and quoted without proof; the book proves it for `L^p`
bounded martingales (Theorem F.5) and, for `L¹` bounded martingales, only `lim sup |f_n| < ∞` (Corollary F.4); cited
as "the martingale theorem" at P3:4082 and in App. J, P3:5382). An `L¹` bounded martingale converges almost
everywhere.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa6): any probability space and any filtration, without (F.2), which the theorem does not
need (a documented true generalization: App. J applies it to dyadic filtrations on `ℝ`); complex-valued; "`L¹`
bounded" is `sup_n ‖f_n‖₁ < ∞` in `[0, ∞]`; "converges" is convergence to a finite limit, `f_n(x) → c ∈ ℂ`, for
almost every `x`. Near miss: Mathlib `MeasureTheory.Submartingale.exists_ae_tendsto_of_bdd` (real submartingales,
the same conclusion; the complex statement follows through the real and imaginary parts, a derivation, not a
renaming). -/
theorem doob_martingale_theorem {X : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace X} {P : Measure X} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0} {f : ℕ → X → ℂ} (hf : Martingale f ℱ P) (hbdd : ⨆ n, eLpNorm (f n) 1 P < ⊤) :
    ∀ᵐ x ∂P, ∃ c : ℂ, Tendsto (fun n => f n x) atTop (𝓝 c) := by
  sorry

/-- **Lemma F.3** (App. F, P3:3951–3993; the weak-`L¹` estimate for the maximal function; cited in the proof of
Corollary F.4, P3:3999, as (F.6) in the proof of Theorem F.5, P3:4021, in the proof of Theorem F.6, P3:4126, and in
Exercise F.1(c)). Let `{f_n}` be a martingale such that `sup_n ‖f_n‖₁ = M < ∞`. Set `f*(x) = sup_n |f_n(x)|`. Then
for all `λ > 0`, (F.6) `P(f* > λ) ≤ M/λ`.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa6): any filtration, without (F.2), which the proof does not use (a documented true
generalization); complex-valued; `M` and `f*` take values in `[0, ∞]` (`f*` written inline as `⨆ n, ‖f n x‖ₑ`) and
the estimate is compared in `[0, ∞]`; `λ` is named `lam` (`λ` is reserved in Lean), and `λ > 0` keeps `M/λ` off a
zero denominator. Near miss: Mathlib `MeasureTheory.maximal_ineq` (nonnegative real submartingales, finitely many
indices, the event `{ε ≤ max_{k≤n} f_k}` bounded by an integral over that event). (F.6) was checked against the
equation image. -/
theorem lemmaF_3 {X : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace X} {P : Measure X} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0} {f : ℕ → X → ℂ} (hf : Martingale f ℱ P) {M : ℝ≥0∞}
    (hM : ⨆ n, eLpNorm (f n) 1 P = M) (hMfin : M < ⊤) {lam : ℝ} (hlam : 0 < lam) :
    P {x | ENNReal.ofReal lam < ⨆ n, ‖f n x‖ₑ} ≤ M / ENNReal.ofReal lam := by
  sorry

/-- **Corollary F.4** (App. F, P3:3997–4005). If `{f_n}` is a martingale such that `sup_n ‖f_n‖₁ < ∞`, then
`lim sup |f_n| < ∞` almost everywhere.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa6): any filtration, without (F.2), which the proof does not use (a documented true
generalization); complex-valued; the `lim sup` of `|f_n(x)|` is taken in `[0, ∞]` (`Filter.limsup` of `‖f n x‖ₑ`),
so no junk value of a real `lim sup` of an unbounded sequence is read. Near miss: Mathlib
`MeasureTheory.Submartingale.exists_ae_tendsto_of_bdd` (real submartingales, a stronger conclusion). -/
theorem corF_4 {X : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace X} {P : Measure X} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0} {f : ℕ → X → ℂ} (hf : Martingale f ℱ P) (hbdd : ⨆ n, eLpNorm (f n) 1 P < ⊤) :
    ∀ᵐ x ∂P, Filter.limsup (fun n => ‖f n x‖ₑ) atTop < ⊤ := by
  sorry

/-- **Theorem F.5** (App. F, P3:4007–4039; the case of `L^p` bounded martingales of Doob's theorem, "the martingale
theorem" used at P3:4082 and in App. J). Let `p > 1` and let `{f_n}` be a martingale satisfying
`sup_n ‖f_n‖_p < ∞`. Let `f` be the weak limit provided by Lemma F.2 (printed "Lemma F.3" [misprint]: Lemma F.3 is
the maximal inequality, and the proof starts from Lemma F.2's conclusion `f_n = E(f∣ℳ_n)`; root Corrections list).
Then `f_n(x) → f(x)` for almost all `x`.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa6): (F.2) `⨆ n, ℱ n = m0` is a hypothesis, since the proof uses it (the functions that
are `ℳ_n`-measurable for some `n` are dense in `L^p` "by (F.2)", P3:4033); complex-valued; `p ∈ (1, ∞]`: `p = ∞` is
allowed [reading] ("By Hölder's inequality we may assume `p < ∞`"); "the weak limit provided by Lemma F.2" is any
`f ∈ L^p` with `f_n = E(f∣ℳ_n)` almost everywhere for all `n`, which is Lemma F.2's conclusion without its
weak-convergence clause: under (F.2) this determines `f` almost everywhere (two such functions have the same
integrals over the sets of the π-system `⋃_n ℳ_n`, which generates `ℳ`), so the weak-convergence clause, a
consequence (for `p = ∞`, weak-* convergence), is not repeated. Near miss: Mathlib
`MeasureTheory.Integrable.tendsto_ae_condExp` (real `f`, measurable for `⨆ n, ℱ n`). -/
theorem thmF_5 {X : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace X} {P : Measure X} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    {ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0} (hgen : ⨆ n, ℱ n = m0) {f : ℕ → X → ℂ} (hf : Martingale f ℱ P) {p : ℝ≥0∞} (hp : 1 < p)
    (hbdd : ⨆ n, eLpNorm (f n) p P < ⊤) {g : X → ℂ} (hg : MemLp g p P) (hgf : ∀ n, f n =ᵐ[P] P[g | ℱ n]) :
    ∀ᵐ x ∂P, Tendsto (fun n => f n x) atTop (𝓝 (g x)) := by
  sorry

/-- **Definition** (the probability space of the walk (F.9), App. F, P3:4059–4065). Let
`X = {(e^{iθ₁}, e^{iθ₂}, …)}` be an infinite product of unit circles, let `ℳ` be the Borel σ-algebra on `X` and let
`P` be the infinite product measure `∏ dθ_j/2π`.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa7): the point `(e^{iθ₁}, e^{iθ₂}, …)` of `X` is recorded by its angles, `θ : ℕ → ℝ`, where
the coordinate `θ j` is the book's `θ_{j+1}` (0-indexing); each factor is the uniform law on `[0, 2π)`, Mathlib's
conditional measure `volume[|Set.Ico 0 (2 * π)]`, which is `dθ/2π` on `[0, 2π)` (leader ruling Fa8/Fb6: no
helper). `e^{iθ} ↦ θ ∈ [0, 2π)` is a Borel isomorphism of the circle onto `[0, 2π)` carrying the normalized arc
length to `dθ/2π`, and for a countable product of second countable spaces the Borel σ-algebra is the product
σ-algebra; so `(X, ℳ, P)` is isomorphic to `ℕ → ℝ` with `MeasurableSpace.pi` and this measure, which is carried by
`[0, 2π)^ℕ`. `Measure.infinitePi` is Mathlib's product of an arbitrary family of probability measures; it is defined
by cases on `∀ i, IsProbabilityMeasure (μ i)`, with the value `0` otherwise, and here every factor is a probability
measure (`volume (Ico 0 (2π)) = 2π ∈ (0, ∞)`), so the definition is the genuine product measure and its junk branch
is never taken. -/
def circleWalkMeasure : Measure (ℕ → ℝ) :=
  Measure.infinitePi (fun _ : ℕ => (volume : Measure ℝ)[|Set.Ico 0 (2 * π)])

/-- **Definition** (the σ-algebras `ℳ_n` of the walk (F.9), App. F, P3:4065). Set `ℳ₀ = {∅, X}` and let `ℳ_n` be
the smallest σ-algebra for which `θ₁, θ₂, …, θ_n` are measurable.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa7): `ℳ_n` is the supremum of the σ-algebras generated by the coordinates `θ j`, `j < n`
(the book's `θ₁, …, θ_n`); for `n = 0` the supremum is empty and `ℳ₀ = ⊥ = {∅, X}`, as in the book. It is a genuine
`MeasureTheory.Filtration` of the product σ-algebra: its two proof obligations (monotone in `n`; each `ℳ_n` is a
sub-σ-algebra of `MeasurableSpace.pi`) are proved in the definition. Mathlib's `MeasureTheory.Filtration.piLE`
(coordinates `≤ n`) is not used: its `ℳ₀` is `σ(θ 0)`, not trivial. -/
def circleWalkFiltration : Filtration ℕ (MeasurableSpace.pi : MeasurableSpace (ℕ → ℝ)) where
  seq n := ⨆ (j : ℕ) (_ : j < n), MeasurableSpace.comap (fun θ : ℕ → ℝ => θ j) inferInstance
  mono' _ _ hnm := biSup_mono fun _ hj => lt_of_lt_of_le hj hnm
  le' _ := iSup₂_le fun j _ => (measurable_pi_apply j).comap_le

/-- **Definition** (the martingale (F.9), App. F, P3:4041–4069). Let `Ω` be a bounded domain in `ℂ` and for each
`z ∈ Ω` let `Γ(z) = {w : |w − z| = ½ dist(z, ∂Ω)}`. For fixed `z₀ ∈ Ω` take `z_{n+1} ∈ Γ(z_n)`, `n ≥ 0`, such
that `z_{n+1} − z_n` is uniformly distributed; on the probability space `X` this is
(F.9) `z_{n+1} = z_n + ½ dist(z_n, ∂Ω) e^{iθ_{n+1}}`.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa7): recursion on `n`, started at `z₀`; `dist(z, ∂Ω)` is `Metric.infDist z (frontier Ω)`;
the angle `θ n` (0-indexed, the book's `θ_{n+1}`) enters the step to `z_{n+1}`, so `z_n` depends on
`θ 0, …, θ (n − 1)` only; `Γ(z)` enters only through (F.9), and "uniformly distributed on `Γ(z_n)`" is the uniform
law of `θ n` under `circleWalkMeasure`. Defined for every `Ω` and `z₀`; the book's discourse is a bounded domain `Ω`
and `z₀ ∈ Ω` (then `∂Ω ≠ ∅` and every `z_n` lies in `Ω`), which every statement assumes. (F.9) was checked against
the equation image. -/
def circleWalk (Ω : Set ℂ) (z₀ : ℂ) : ℕ → (ℕ → ℝ) → ℂ
  | 0 => fun _ => z₀
  | n + 1 => fun θ => circleWalk Ω z₀ n θ +
      (((1 / 2 : ℝ) * Metric.infDist (circleWalk Ω z₀ n θ) (frontier Ω) : ℝ) : ℂ) * Complex.exp (θ n * I)

/-- **Unlabelled** (App. F, P3:4071–4075; it is why "the martingale theorem" applies at P3:4082, and Theorem F.6
calls `z_∞` "the limit of the martingale (F.9)", P3:4102). `{z_n}` is a martingale relative to `{ℳ_n}` because
`(1/2π) ∫₀^{2π} e^{iθ} dθ = 0`.
Modelling: the setting of (F.9) (P3:4041–4047): `Ω` a bounded domain of `ℂ` (open, connected, bounded) and
`z₀ ∈ Ω`; a complex-valued martingale (leader ruling Fa6) for `circleWalkFiltration` and `circleWalkMeasure`. -/
theorem circleWalk_martingale {Ω : Set ℂ} (hΩo : IsOpen Ω) (hΩc : IsConnected Ω) (hΩb : Bornology.IsBounded Ω)
    {z₀ : ℂ} (hz₀ : z₀ ∈ Ω) :
    Martingale (circleWalk Ω z₀) circleWalkFiltration circleWalkMeasure := by
  sorry

/-- **Unlabelled** (App. F, P3:4082–4086; used by the statement of Theorem F.6, "`z_∞` is the limit of the
martingale (F.9)", and at P3:4180–4190). Because `Ω` is bounded, the martingale theorem ensures that
`z_∞ = lim_n z_n` exists almost everywhere on `X`.
Modelling: the setting of (F.9); "exists" is `∃ w, Tendsto (fun n ↦ z_n(θ)) atTop (𝓝 w)` for
`circleWalkMeasure`-almost every `θ` (no `limUnder`). -/
theorem circleWalk_tendsto_ae {Ω : Set ℂ} (hΩo : IsOpen Ω) (hΩc : IsConnected Ω) (hΩb : Bornology.IsBounded Ω)
    {z₀ : ℂ} (hz₀ : z₀ ∈ Ω) :
    ∀ᵐ θ ∂circleWalkMeasure, ∃ w : ℂ, Tendsto (fun n => circleWalk Ω z₀ n θ) atTop (𝓝 w) := by
  sorry

/-- **Unlabelled** (App. F, P3:4088–4094; used by Theorem F.6, which measures `{z_∞ ∈ E}` for `E ⊂ ∂Ω`: "Kakutani's
famous theorem identifies the hitting distribution of `z_∞` with the harmonic measure of `z₀` on `∂Ω`"). Clearly
`z_∞ ∈ Ω̄`, and because `dist(z_n, ∂Ω) = 2|z_{n+1} − z_n| → 0`, we have `z_∞ ∈ ∂Ω` whenever `z_∞` exists.
Modelling: the setting of (F.9); deterministic, for every `θ` at which the limit exists ("whenever `z_∞` exists");
`∂Ω` is the frontier in `ℂ`. -/
theorem circleWalk_limit_mem_frontier {Ω : Set ℂ} (hΩo : IsOpen Ω) (hΩc : IsConnected Ω)
    (hΩb : Bornology.IsBounded Ω) {z₀ : ℂ} (hz₀ : z₀ ∈ Ω) {θ : ℕ → ℝ} {w : ℂ}
    (hw : Tendsto (fun n => circleWalk Ω z₀ n θ) atTop (𝓝 w)) :
    w ∈ frontier Ω := by
  sorry

/-- **Theorem F.6** (Kakutani; App. F, P3:4094–4102, "Kakutani's famous theorem"; cited at P3:4104, 4110, 4192, in
Ch. III, P1:3278, "Appendix F below includes Kakutani's theorem for the discrete version of Brownian motion", and
through its restatement in Ch. X, Exercise 15, P3:2207). Let `z₀ ∈ Ω` and let `E ⊂ ∂Ω` be a Borel set. Then
(F.10) `ω(z₀, E, Ω) = P(z_∞ ∈ E)`, where `z_∞` is the limit of the martingale (F.9) started at `z₀`.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa8): `Ω` is the bounded domain of P3:4041 (open, connected, bounded); `ω` is Wiener's
harmonic measure (Ch. III §5; leader rulings D1, B5: an arbitrary bounded domain), a measure on `ℂ*` applied to the
plane Borel set `E` as `toExt E`; `Cap(ℂ* \ Ω) > 0` holds automatically for bounded `Ω` (`ℂ* \ Ω` contains the
complement of a disc) and is not added. `P(z_∞ ∈ E)` is the `circleWalkMeasure`-measure of the set of `θ` for which
`z_n(θ)` converges to a point of `E` (no `limUnder`); by `circleWalk_tendsto_ae` the limit exists almost
everywhere, so this is the book's event. The two sides are compared in `[0, ∞]`. (F.10) was checked against the
equation image. -/
theorem thmF_6 {Ω : Set ℂ} (hΩo : IsOpen Ω) (hΩc : IsConnected Ω) (hΩb : Bornology.IsBounded Ω) {z₀ : ℂ}
    (hz₀ : z₀ ∈ Ω) {E : Set ℂ} (hE : MeasurableSet E) (hEΩ : E ⊆ frontier Ω) :
    harmonicMeasureWiener (toExt Ω) (z₀ : ExtPlane) (toExt E) =
      circleWalkMeasure {θ | ∃ w ∈ E, Tendsto (fun n => circleWalk Ω z₀ n θ) atTop (𝓝 w)} := by
  sorry

/-- **Definition** (Brownian motion started at `z₀`, App. F, P3:4142–4166). Let `W` be the set of continuous paths
`B(t)`, `t ≥ 0`, in the plane having common initial point `B(0) = z₀`. By a theorem of Wiener there is a
probability measure `P` on `W` with respect to which `B(t)` will almost surely meet any circle
`Γ_r(z₀) = {z : |z − z₀| = r}` at some finite time, the location of the first hitting of `Γ_r(z₀)` is uniformly
distributed, all the increments `B(t + s) − B(s)` have the same distribution as `B(t) − z₀`, and the increments
over the intervals of `0 ≤ t₀ < t₁ < ⋯ < t_n` are stochastically independent. With an appropriate σ-algebra `ℳ`,
the measure space `(W, ℳ, P)` is called Brownian motion.
Modelling (leader rulings D5, Fa9): the property of a process `B : ℝ≥0 → Ω' → ℂ` on a measure space `(Ω', P)` of
being a planar Brownian motion started at `z₀`: the real and imaginary parts of `B − z₀` are standard real Brownian
motions (Mathlib `ProbabilityTheory.IsBrownianReal`: the Gaussian finite-dimensional laws of Brownian motion, which
force `P` to be a probability measure and `B(0) = z₀` almost surely, and almost surely continuous paths), and the
real and imaginary parts of `B` are independent as path-valued random variables (`ProbabilityTheory.IndepFun` for
the product σ-algebra on `ℝ≥0 → ℝ`; that σ-algebra is generated by the measurable cylinders, a π-system (Mathlib
`MeasureTheory.generateFrom_measurableCylinders`, `MeasureTheory.isPiSystem_measurableCylinders`), so this is the
independence of all pairs of finite-dimensional marginals, the standard notion and nothing stronger). The book's list
of properties determines the law only up to a linear change of the time scale (independent stationary increments,
continuity and the uniform first hits force a centred isotropic Gaussian law); the standard normalization, variance
`t` for each coordinate, is used, and exit distributions do not depend on the time scale. "Brownian path" (a random
function `B(t)`) is a term and is not defined separately. -/
def IsPlanarBrownianFrom {Ω' : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω'] (z₀ : ℂ) (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω' → ℂ) (P : Measure Ω') :
    Prop :=
  ProbabilityTheory.IsBrownianReal (fun t w => (B t w - z₀).re) P ∧
    ProbabilityTheory.IsBrownianReal (fun t w => (B t w - z₀).im) P ∧
    ProbabilityTheory.IndepFun (fun w t => (B t w).re) (fun w t => (B t w).im) P

/-- **Wiener's theorem** (App. F, P3:4142, "By a theorem of Wiener", quoted without proof; the book's `P` is used at
P3:4166–4198). There is a probability measure `P` on the set `W` of continuous paths `B(t)`, `t ≥ 0`, in the plane
with `B(0) = z₀`, with respect to which `B(t)` is Brownian motion.
Modelling (leader ruling Fa9): `W` is the subtype `{w : ℝ≥0 → ℂ // Continuous w ∧ w 0 = z₀}` with the subspace
σ-algebra of the product σ-algebra ("an appropriate σ-algebra"), and `B(t)` is the coordinate process `w ↦ w t`; the
conclusion is the Mathlib-normalized Brownian law (`IsPlanarBrownianFrom`). The book's listed properties (every
circle `Γ_r(z₀)` is hit almost surely, the first hit is uniformly distributed, the increments are stationary and
independent) are consequences and are not stated. Mathlib has no existence theorem for Brownian motion: its
`Probability/BrownianMotion` files hold the predicates `ProbabilityTheory.IsPreBrownianReal`,
`ProbabilityTheory.IsBrownianReal` and their invariances. -/
theorem wiener_brownian_motion (z₀ : ℂ) :
    ∃ P : Measure {w : ℝ≥0 → ℂ // Continuous w ∧ w 0 = z₀},
      IsProbabilityMeasure P ∧ IsPlanarBrownianFrom z₀ (fun t w => w.1 t) P := by
  sorry

/-- **Unlabelled** (App. F, P3:4192–4198; the book's restatement of Theorem F.6, used by name in Ch. X, Exercise 15,
P3:2207, "By Kakutani's theorem", and described in Ch. III's introduction, P1:3274, "The Brownian motion approach,
originally from Kakutani [1944a], identifies `ω(z, E, Ω)` with the probability that a randomly moving particle,
starting at `z`, first hits `∂Ω` in the set `E`"). Theorem F.6 can be restated as `ω(z₀, E, Ω) = P(B(τ_∞) ∈ E)`,
`τ_∞ = inf {t : B(t) ∈ ∂Ω}`, or in words, the harmonic measure at `z₀` of the Borel set `E` is the probability that
Brownian motion, started at `z₀`, first exits `Ω` in `E` (the book prints `w` for `ω`; checked against the equation
image).
Modelling (leader ruling Fa9): the setting of Theorem F.6; Brownian motion started at `z₀` is any realization `B` on
any measure space (`IsPlanarBrownianFrom z₀ B P`): the probability depends only on the law, and the canonical
`(W, P)` of Wiener's theorem (`wiener_brownian_motion`) is a special case; `τ_∞` is
`MeasureTheory.hittingAfter B (frontier Ω) 0` (values in `WithTop ℝ≥0`, `⊤` when `∂Ω` is never hit); the event
`{B(τ_∞) ∈ E}` is `{∃ t, τ_∞ = t ∧ B(t) ∈ E}`, which includes `τ_∞ < ∞`; compared in `[0, ∞]`. -/
theorem thmF_6_brownian {Ω : Set ℂ} (hΩo : IsOpen Ω) (hΩc : IsConnected Ω) (hΩb : Bornology.IsBounded Ω)
    {z₀ : ℂ} (hz₀ : z₀ ∈ Ω) {E : Set ℂ} (hE : MeasurableSet E) (hEΩ : E ⊆ frontier Ω) {Ω' : Type*}
    [MeasurableSpace Ω'] {P : Measure Ω'} {B : ℝ≥0 → Ω' → ℂ} (hB : IsPlanarBrownianFrom z₀ B P) :
    harmonicMeasureWiener (toExt Ω) (z₀ : ExtPlane) (toExt E) =
      P {w | ∃ t : ℝ≥0, hittingAfter B (frontier Ω) 0 w = t ∧ B t w ∈ E} := by
  sorry

end GarnettMarshall2005
